.TH EUCLID-CLI-EKV-DELETE-ITEM 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.73" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ekv-delete-item \- remove one item by its key
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ekv delete-item
.BR \-\-table | \-t
.I TABLE
.RB { \-\-key | \-k
.I JSON
|
.B \-\-key\-file
.IR PATH }
.SH DESCRIPTION
Removes one item and says whether there was one to remove.
.PP
Deleting something that is not there is not an error \- the outcome
asked for is the outcome you get \- so the answer carries
.I deleted
as true or false rather than failing. That is what makes it safe to run
twice.
.PP
The key is an object of the table's key attributes, exactly as for
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-get-item (1).
There is no delete-by-query and no delete-by-prefix: removing a
partition means reading it with
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-query (1)
and deleting the items, or deleting the table.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-table " " \fITABLE\fR
Table to remove from. Required.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " " \fIJSON\fR
The key attributes, as a JSON object.
.TP
.BR \-\-key\-file " " \fIPATH\fR
A file holding the key as JSON.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The request was carried out; whether an item was removed is in the
answer.
.TP
.B 1
No such table, or the key was missing an attribute or carried one of
the wrong type.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli ekv delete-item --table suppliers --key '{"supplierId":"4711"}'
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ekv (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-get-item (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-delete-table (1)
